An important high-level finding first: the code, the OpenAPI spec, and the acceptance test are three inconsistent versions of the identity model. The code currently produces two separate fields (`customerCode` = `<REGION>-<HASH8>`, `membershipNumber` = `<customerCode>-M<YY>`) plus a `checkDigit`; the OpenAPI spec still documents the OLD `<LAST3>-<NNNN>` sequence format; and the acceptance test expects a future unified `memberId` = `<REGION><FY><HASH8><CHK>` with `customerCode`/`membershipNumber` removed. Details below.

---

## 1. customerCode — computed in `AssignCustomerCode`
`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/AssignCustomerCode.java`

Format: `<REGION>-<HASH8>` where REGION comes from `Locality.of(postcode, city)` and HASH8 = first 4 bytes (8 UPPER-case hex) of SHA-256 of `telephone + lastName`.

```java
public class AssignCustomerCode {
    public void service(@Val Owner owner) {
        String region = Locality.of(owner.getPostcode(), owner.getCity());
        String hash8 = hash8(owner.getTelephone() + owner.getLastName());
        owner.setCustomerCode(region + "-" + hash8);
    }
    private static String hash8(String value) {
        try {
            byte[] digest = MessageDigest.getInstance("SHA-256").digest(value.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8));
            StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
            for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
                sb.append(String.format("%02X", digest[i]));   // %02X => upper hex, 4 bytes = 8 chars
            }
            return sb.toString();
        } catch (NoSuchAlgorithmException e) { throw new IllegalStateException(e); }
    }
}
```
Note: the class Javadoc (lines 11-16) says "the first 8 upper-case hex characters of the SHA-256 of the normalized telephone concatenated with the last name" — but the code uses `owner.getTelephone()`, not a separately-normalized value (telephone is normalized earlier in the pipeline by `NormalizeOwnerTelephone`).

## 2. membershipNumber — computed in `MembershipNumber`
`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/mapper/MembershipNumber.java`

Format: `<customerCode>-M<YY>`, YY = last two digits of the registration date's fiscal year.
```java
public static String of(String customerCode, LocalDate registrationDate) {
    return String.format("%s-M%02d", customerCode, FiscalYear.of(registrationDate) % 100);
}
```
Wired into the response DTO in `OwnerMapper` line 27, and also recomputed for the audit log in `SaveOwner` line 22.

## 3. checkDigit (Luhn) — computed in `CheckDigit`
`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/mapper/CheckDigit.java`

Single Luhn digit over the digits contained in `customerCode` (non-digits skipped). Full method:
```java
public static int of(String customerCode) {
    int sum = 0;
    boolean dbl = true;
    for (int i = customerCode.length() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
        char c = customerCode.charAt(i);
        if (c < '0' || c > '9') { continue; }
        int d = c - '0';
        if (dbl) { d *= 2; if (d > 9) { d -= 9; } }
        sum += d;
        dbl = !dbl;
    }
    return (10 - (sum % 10)) % 10;
}
```
Wired into the DTO in `OwnerMapper` line 28. A duplicate reference implementation exists in the test base `AcceptanceBase.luhn(...)` (lines 301-321).

## 4. region-and-hash / HASH8 (SHA-256 over telephone+lastName, first 8 hex)
This is the `hash8(...)` method inside `AssignCustomerCode` (see item 1, lines 25-36). There is NO class literally named `regionAndHash`/`region-and-hash`; that concept is the `<REGION>-<HASH8>` customerCode.

Note there is a SEPARATE, different SHA-256 identity key — do not confuse it:
`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/mapper/IdentityKey.java`
```java
public static String of(String telephone, String email, String lastName) {
    String key = (telephone == null ? "" : telephone) + "|"
            + (email == null ? "" : email.toLowerCase()) + "|" + Soundex.of(lastName);
    // full lower-case SHA-256 hex of key
}
```
`IdentityKey` hashes `telephone|lowerEmail|soundex(lastName)` (full hex) and is the duplicate-detection key — different input and different length from customerCode's HASH8. Wired in `OwnerMapper` line 26.

Also related: `HouseholdId` (`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/mapper/HouseholdId.java`) — first 12 lower-hex chars of SHA-256 over `normalizedLastName|postcode`.

## 5. Region code — `Locality`
`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/mapper/Locality.java`

Postcode-range first (NSW 2000-2099, VIC 3000-3099, QLD 4000-4099), else city table (Sydney→NSW, Melbourne→VIC, Brisbane→QLD), else `UNKNOWN`. Full class read; `of(postcode, city)` is what `AssignCustomerCode` calls. Region ranges are duplicated in `RejectInvalidPostcode.java` (lines 19-20) and in the test base `AcceptanceBase.REGION_POSTCODES` (lines 236-237) / `CITY_REGION` (lines 228-229).

## 6. Fiscal year — `FiscalYear`
`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/mapper/FiscalYear.java`

FY starts 1 July; July-Dec roll into next year. `of(date)` returns 4-digit year; `label(date)` returns `FY<YY>`. Used by `MembershipNumber.of` (item 2) and by `OwnerMapper` line 35 for the `fiscalYear` DTO field.

## 7. Collision handling — `DeduplicateCustomerCode`
`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/DeduplicateCustomerCode.java`

On customerCode collision, appends `-<n>` starting at n=2 with smallest n making it unique. Compares against all existing owners via `ownerRepository.findAll()`. Full method:
```java
public void service(@Val Owner owner, OwnerRepository ownerRepository) {
    Set<String> taken = new HashSet<>();
    for (Owner existing : ownerRepository.findAll()) { taken.add(existing.getCustomerCode()); }
    String base = owner.getCustomerCode();
    String candidate = base;
    for (int n = 2; taken.contains(candidate); n++) { candidate = base + "-" + n; }
    owner.setCustomerCode(candidate);
